Unreleased
---
Feature
- `FluentButton`, the command the page is built out of: three kinds say three
  weights of command (standard, accent, subtle), a face carries words or an
  icon or both, and the press never moves it.
- `FluentDivider`, one hairline that separates: full across the edge it was
  given or inset past the indent the content beside it keeps.
- `FluentCheckBox`, a choice that stands beside other choices: a box of
  twenty with the tick drawn, three states when the application asks for the
  third one, and the whole row answering the pointer.
- `FluentRadioButton`, one choice of several: the accent's ring and dot when
  it is in force, and the brothers that share its parent out of force with
  it.
- `FluentComboBox`, a choice the page has already made: the face shows what
  is in force, the list opens below and turns above when below has no room,
  and it keeps inside the screen when neither side has room; the keyboard
  walks the list and answers with Enter.
- `FluentProgressBar`, a track four tall that says how far along something
  is: a fill that ends at the value over the range the application gave, or
  a block that slides and only says the waiting has not stopped; asked for
  less motion, the block holds still in view instead of vanishing.
- `FluentToolTip`, a note that waits beside its anchor and opens when the
  pointer or the keyboard arrives and stays: a surface in the page's ink
  saying its words in the page's colour, standing on whichever side of the
  anchor it was told, taking no mouse and no focus while it is up.
- `FluentTextBox`, one line of text in a face the page can see: the typing
  stays QLineEdit's and the face is the family's - the box slightly inked
  under a hairline, the prompt that goes when the words come, an icon before
  them for what the box is for, a clear at the right that empties the box
  and says so, and the accent along the foot while the keyboard is in.
- `FluentMenuFlyout`, the surface a list of commands opens into, drawn by the
  family on the shared popup surface instead of styled into a QMenu: rows of
  thirty-two carrying actions - words, an icon, the shortcut beside them, a
  check on the one in force, a hairline between groups, a heading naming
  them - and the keyboard walking the ones that can be worked.
- `FluentPasswordBox`, the text box keeping a secret: the characters it
  holds are drawn as the echo until the eye at the trailing edge is held
  down - and they are the echo again the moment it is let go.
- `FluentAutoSuggestBox`, the text box that answers while it is being written
  into: the writing drives the flyout's list - what still matches is in it -
  and Enter, or a click, takes what is in hand into the box, while Escape
  puts the list away without touching what was written.

0.1.2
---
Bugfix
- A window material asked of a window that had already handed itself over to a
  qxwindow agent used to make a second agent for that window. Two agents answer
  the same hit test, and the newer one knows nothing about the caption the
  window draws - so the caption stopped moving the window, which is the one
  thing a frameless window with a caption of its own is for. The material now
  finds the agent that is already on the window and asks that one, and only
  makes an agent when there is none. Nothing about this is observable on a
  platform that has no materials: the material still answers no there, as
  before.
- The panel a folded pane opens beside an icon used to stay up until one of its
  own rows was chosen. It is a popup, and a popup is put away by the first press
  outside it - but only while the mouse grab Qt takes for it is still there, and
  a window that moves itself by a caption of its own can take that grab back.
  The panel now also ends on a press anywhere other than itself, and when it
  stops being the window in front. The press that ends it is not swallowed: it
  is still the press it was.

Feature
- FluentNavigationView now has the second shape WinUI's navigation view has: a
  destination can hold destinations under it, which is the difference between a
  pane of nineteen places and a pane of three things that each hold six. It is
  still one control with one list - not a list control beside a tree control -
  because there is no model here to be flat or nested: `addItem(text, icon,
  parent)` puts a destination under another one and the pane draws it there,
  stepping it in by its depth.
- The destinations keep the indexes they have whatever is open. An index is a
  name rather than a place on the pane, so folding a group does not renumber
  anything that follows it, and `items()` still answers every destination the
  view holds, folded or not. Naming a folded destination with
  `setCurrentIndex()` opens the groups above it, rather than putting it in force
  somewhere the reader cannot see.
- A parent is a destination like any other. Pressing it turns it over and goes
  there in the same press, which is WinUI's SelectsOnInvoked; an application
  that wants a group to be only a group says so by not connecting to the
  action, which is what an action that is not connected means everywhere else.
  A group opens and closes from the arrow at the trailing edge of its parent,
  from Right and Left, from `setExpanded()`, and from `expandedChanged()` for
  whoever needs to know.
- Folded, the pane is its icons and there is no room beside one for what stands
  under it - so what stands under it steps out instead, into a panel over the
  page whose rows are the pane's rows and whose indentation is the pane's
  indentation. That is WinUI's LeftCompact reading of a hierarchy, and the
  reason a fold on a hierarchical pane is not a dead end: a destination folded
  away is still a destination that can be reached. Pressing a parent in that
  shape is a way in rather than a journey, since the panel is what the press
  asked for.
- A group remembers being open, and a destination added under another one
  starts visible: a control whose new destinations arrived folded would be
  hiding the thing it was just given. A parent that is carrying the destination
  in force somewhere under it says so with the bar at the leading edge and none
  of the wash, because it is not the destination itself - WinUI's
  IsChildSelected.
- `addHeader()` is unchanged by this: a heading is room on the pane over a
  group of top-level destinations, not a destination and not a level. A group of
  destinations is what a parent destination is for.

Optimize
- The command faces are one drawing now. The command bar and the two buttons
  that stand alone had each their own copy of the chevron, the measures and the
  opacities a command is drawn with - three answers to "what does a command
  look like", which is two too many for things that sit in the same row. They
  draw from one place now, and the bar takes the shape the buttons brought:
  its chevron was drawn a shade deeper than theirs, and its seam - the line
  between the two halves of a split command - a shade lighter, both differences
  of history rather than of promise.

0.1.1
---
Feature
- FluentContentDialog, a question asked over a dimmed page. Qt's QDialog is a
  window with a title bar and a QDialogButtonBox under it, and what a Fluent
  dialog is made of is none of those: it is a card in the middle of the page it
  was opened over, on a veil that says the page is not the thing to look at now,
  with no chrome of the platform's anywhere in it - so the veil, the shadow of
  the 64 level and the rounded card are drawn here, and the window underneath is
  still a QDialog, because modality, Escape, exec() and a result code are the
  toolkit's and writing them again would only produce a worse copy. The card is
  painted in the page's own colour and what lifts it is the veil, which is the
  whole reason a scrim is drawn rather than a border; the card keeps the reach
  of its own shadow off the edge of the page, so a page too short for the
  dialogue shortens the card rather than letting the shadow be cut. At most
  three buttons exist, one of each role, and each is made the first time it is
  asked for - a dialog that never asks for one has none. They are the toolkit's
  QPushButtons and the dialog does not answer for them: pressing one emits
  clicked() and nothing else happens, because whether the question was answered,
  and which way that counts, is the application's - a dialog sits on top of the
  write it is asking about, and a control that closed itself would take that
  decision away. Fluent reads the row from the affirmative, so the answer comes
  before the way out of being asked, and Return reaches the affirmative and
  nothing else.
- FluentToggleSplitButton, a command that stays down with a menu beside it:
  doing and choosing more are two things, and this is the shape that says so.
  The left half is the command - it goes down and stays down, and the face is
  washed in the accent while it is in force - and the right half is the chevron
  that opens the menu. QToolButton's MenuButtonPopup has the same two halves
  and means something else by them, and neither it nor a button with a menu
  beside it can say "this is on" without the menu being the answer to why. Here
  a press is answered by the half it started on and by nothing else: a press
  that begins on the body and is released over the chevron has not chosen more,
  and one that begins on the chevron has not done the command - the seam
  between them is drawn so that a reader does not have to learn it. The
  keyboard has no halves to aim at, so the keys say it instead: Space and Enter
  toggle, Down and F4 open the menu. For an assistive tool it is a check box,
  because the question it answers is whether the command is in force.
- FluentDropDownButton, a command that is a menu: the whole face opens it. Qt's
  QToolButton can be told to behave this way and is then drawn by a style a
  Fluent application replaces anyway, but what it cannot be told is the promise
  the shape makes - that there is nothing here to press and then something else
  to choose. So the face is one thing and a press anywhere on it, chevron
  included, opens the menu; the half that means "do the command" belongs to
  FluentToggleSplitButton, and the two are separate classes because they are
  separate promises. The menu is the button's own child, which is the one
  ownership question this class answers: an application fills it - before the
  menu opens, since aboutToShowMenu() is emitted first - and never owns it. A
  menu left empty is not opened at all, because opening an empty box is a
  promise the button cannot keep. Space, Enter, Down and F4 open it, the
  chevron is drawn rather than typed so the theme can colour it and no
  assistive tool reads it as a character, and the text is the accessible name
  until the application gives a better one. The face is turned by the medium
  radius every command in a bar is drawn with, and both the hover wash and the
  focus ring follow that turn - the ring sits at the middle of an edge and not
  at a corner, which is the case a regression test holds it to.
- FluentNumberBox, a number you can type and step. QSpinBox is a styled widget
  with the arrows stacked in a corner by a style that a Fluent application has
  to replace anyway; this one is drawn - a field that is an opening in the page
  with a hairline round it and an accent bar under it while it has the keyboard,
  and a stepper column of two chevrons, each one a command rather than half of
  a dial. The text is a QLineEdit, so selection, the cursor, the clipboard and
  an input method behave the way the platform taught the user to expect. The
  rule that makes it a number box is the one thing Qt's own spin box leaves to
  the application: the value is the truth and the text is a way of saying it -
  text that is not a number never reaches the value, and leaving the field puts
  the text back to what the value is.
- FluentNavigationView scrolls when its destinations do not fit, which a pane
  that is a column of rows has to: past the room it has, a list that cannot move
  is a list whose end does not exist. The menu button stays where it is - it is
  the pane's header rather than its first row - and what moves is the column of
  destinations under it, clipped to that room so a row scrolled half way out is
  half a row and not a second header. The wheel is asked what it moved instead of
  being assumed to have notches in it: a trackpad reports pixels and a wheel
  reports eighths of a turn, and a notch is the rows the platform's own setting
  asks for - so the pane moves as fast as the rest of the desktop does. The bar
  is the overlay one a Fluent pane wears, three wide with no track under it, as
  long as the part of the list in sight and darker while it is being held; it is
  the answer to "is there more", so it is drawn whenever there is, rather than
  only while the pointer is over it. The keyboard keeps its end of it: a
  destination reached with the arrow keys - or chosen by the application - is
  scrolled into the room it is being shown in.

Bugfix
- FluentCommandBar centres its row in the bar it was given rather than in the
  height it asks for. The two are the same height until a layout gives the bar
  more room - and a title bar that puts the bar in a taller row does exactly
  that, so every command in it sat four pixels high, with the caption buttons
  beside it on the middle line. The bar is laid out again when its height
  changes and not only when its width does.
- FluentCommandBar follows the actions it shows. It draws them itself - the
  wash, the ink, and the fill behind a command that is on - and it repainted
  only on its own events, so an action whose state was set somewhere else - a
  settings page, a menu, a shortcut - went on looking the way it looked before,
  until the pointer happened to cross it. A command bar that answers for a
  toggled command has to be told when the toggle was thrown elsewhere.

0.1.0
---
Feature
- The library opens. It is Fluent's answer to what a control is, drawn in
  Widgets: the corner scale, the type ramp, the elevation scale and the
  duration scale that the controls in here are drawn from, and the controls
  themselves as they land. It is one of three choices an application can make
  - ribbon, Fluent, Material - not a skin for any of them.
- FluentSwitch, the toggle switch: a pill with a knob in it and a sentence
  beside it. Qt has a check box and no switch, and they are not the same
  answer - a switch says what it stands for is in force now, a check box says
  a choice was made and will be collected later. The track fills with the
  accent when it is on and the knob travels on a Fluent duration, which is a
  motion tier: reduce-motion and the theme's scale reach it without the
  control asking about either. A click and the keyboard both toggle, the
  sentence is its accessible name until the application gives it one, and the
  wash and the focus ring stay on the track.
- FluentInfoBar, a message that stays until it is dealt with. A QMessageBox is
  a window the user has to dismiss before doing anything else and a status tip
  leaves before it has been read; what sits between them - something happened,
  it matters, it does not stop you - is what this is. It carries a severity,
  and the severity is what colours it: a theme has a role for a highlight and
  none for "warning", so a bar carries those colours itself. Closing is not
  drawing it differently, it is going away.
- FluentExpander, a header that keeps a body and opens to show it. Qt's answer
  to this is a button next to a widget the application hides and shows itself,
  which is why the two disagree about where the clickable part ends and why
  the movement is instant. Here the header is the control - it is what the
  pointer reaches and what the keyboard stops at - and the body travels out
  from under it on a Fluent duration, or is simply out, under reduce-motion.
- FluentRating, a row of stars filled up to the one the user chose. Qt has no
  rating control, and the two things a rating has to answer are not answered
  by a row of buttons: how much is it now, and what would it be if I clicked
  here. The second is the hover, and it is not decoration - a rating that does
  not show what a click would do is a control the user has to click to find
  out about. The keyboard walks it, because a rating is a value in a range.
- FluentProgressRing, the arc that says how far along something is, or only
  that something is happening. QProgressBar's indeterminate mode is the
  style's to draw, so a Fluent application gets a block sweeping a track. The
  turning here is the one motion in these controls that is not a transition -
  nothing moves from one state to another, it is a loop - so it reads the
  reduce-motion switch itself and stops, because a ring that turns forever is
  what that switch asks not to be shown.
- FluentCard, the surface that sits above the page, and the lift is what says
  how far above. A card is the one place in Fluent where elevation is the
  message rather than decoration, so the shadow is the shadow of the level
  the card was given - painted by the base, cached, and given room: the card
  keeps a margin the size of its own shadow, because contents that sat on the
  shadow of the surface they are on would be sitting on nothing. It reports a
  grouping, so what is inside is announced as a section.
- FluentBadge, the info badge: a dot that says there is something, or a
  number that says how much. Qt has no badge, which is why a count ends up
  painted into a label, a button or a pixmap made at run time - three answers
  that disagree about size, colour and what happens past 99. This one is one
  answer, and it is the accent, because a badge is the application pointing at
  something.
- FluentSlider, the slider: a rail the accent fills up to the thumb, a thumb
  that rides it, and the marks underneath that say where the rail is divided -
  the one thing Fluent's slider says that Material's does not. A drag answers
  at once, because a thumb that lags the pointer is a broken slider; a step
  from the keyboard travels, because there the movement is the feedback. It
  carries a value, so it brings its own accessible face: a role and a state
  cannot say a number, and an assistive tool wants to set it back.
- FluentReveal, the wash that follows the pointer. A hover in most toolkits is
  a flat tint that switches on, which says the pointer is somewhere on this and
  nothing about where; Fluent's answer is a wash strongest under the pointer
  and fading away from it, so a surface looks lit from wherever the user is and
  the lit place travels as they do. It is the hover wash and not an extra one -
  the same ink at the same opacity, given a shape by the pointer - so a control
  that wears it paints no flat wash of its own, and the card does: hovering a
  Fluent card lights it from where the pointer is.
- FluentWindowMaterial, what a Fluent window is made of: Mica, Mica Alt and
  Acrylic, asked for by name and answered with yes or no, because a Windows 10
  cannot give Mica and a Linux has none of them at all - and an application
  that runs anywhere has to be able to ask without being told the answer
  first. They are one slot on a window and not three switches, so asking for
  one turns the others off. What does the asking is qxwindow's window agent,
  which already knows how to reach the window system on each platform; this is
  the Fluent spelling of it and nothing more, and it is left out of the build
  when qxwindow is.
- FluentTeachingTip, a flyout that points at the thing it is about. Qt has a
  tool tip, and a teaching tip is not one: a tool tip is a sentence the toolkit
  shows and hides, it cannot be asked to stay, it holds no button, and it does
  not point at anything - it appears where the pointer happens to be. This one
  is opened by the application on a target of its choosing, stays until it is
  dismissed, carries a title, a sentence and a way out, and reaches towards
  what it is about with a tail - which is what says which of the things on
  screen the sentence belongs to. Which side it sits on is measured, not
  assumed: below the target if the room is there, above it if not, and a side
  can be asked for by name. It is a popup, so clicking anywhere else dismisses
  it, and it says when it went - the application is the one that opened it.
- FluentCommandBar, where the commands of a Fluent application live. An
  application that has chosen Fluent has no ribbon, and this is what it has
  instead: one row of commands, the ones that matter in the order they matter,
  and the ones that do not fit kept behind a button rather than dropped. A
  command here is a QAction - connect to it, disable it, check it - and what
  the bar adds is the answer to what a click does, one of four: a command that
  runs, a command that stays in force, a menu, or both in one, where the
  chevron opens the menu and the rest of it runs the command. Room is the
  bar's to answer: what does not fit goes into the overflow and comes back
  when there is room again, and the row's least is the overflow itself. The
  keyboard walks the row, the wash and the focus ring stay on the one command
  the pointer or the keyboard is on, and what is announced is a toolbar.
- FluentBreadcrumbBar, the path to where the user is, and a way back along it.
  Qt has no breadcrumb, and the nearest thing - a row of flat buttons with ">"
  labels between them - gets three things wrong: the last step is not a
  destination, the separator is part of the text an assistive tool reads, and
  the whole row is announced as a row of buttons rather than as a path. Here
  the last step is where the user already is and answers nothing; the steps
  before it are how to go back; the separator is drawn, not typed; and what is
  announced is the path in one sentence. The wash and the focus ring stay on
  the one step the pointer or the keyboard is on, not on the whole row.
- fluenttokens, the four scales Fluent arrives with, each spelled the way
  Fluent spells it and answered in the shared vocabulary: corners as radii,
  the ramp as sizes with a heavier cut, elevation as the shadow a lift casts,
  and durations mapped onto the motion tiers - which is what makes a control
  that asks here honour the reduce-motion switch and the theme's motion scale
  without asking about either.
- FluentNavigationView, the skeleton a Fluent application is navigated in: a
  column the destinations stand in, and beside it the room where the one in
  force shows up. Qt's answer to this is a list beside a stack that the
  application keeps in step itself, and a QListWidget is drawn by the style in
  whatever shape the style has. Here a destination is a QAction, which is what
  an application already has; the one in force is marked by Fluent's bar at the
  leading edge rather than by filling the row, because a fill reads as a
  pressed button; and the pane can fold itself down to its icons, which is a
  state the view holds rather than a size the application sets - and which is
  why the content beside it moves and the words do not. The wash, the focus
  and what is announced belong to one destination, not to the view: a view has
  no part of it that a ring could be drawn round.
- FluentTabView, several things open at once with one of them showing. It is
  not a navigation view, and the difference is in what a tab is: a destination
  is somewhere you go, a tab is something you have open, and it is still
  holding what you were doing in it when you look at another one - which is why
  a tab carries content of its own here and a destination does not. The one
  being looked at is marked by a line under the tab rather than by the tab
  being a different shape, so a tab is a tab whichever one it is and what
  moves is the line. Closing is asked about rather than done: the view says
  whose close mark was clicked and closes nothing, because whether a tab may be
  closed is the application's to answer and not the view's.

Bugfix
- Where the things in FluentNavigationView and FluentTabView stand is now
  worked out when it is asked for rather than when the room changed. A widget
  that has never been shown is not told it was resized, so a view asked where
  its items or tabs were answered from the geometry it had when it was last
  told - which put the content beside the pane, or under the row, at the size
  the view used to be. A hit test against stale geometry is a lie, and this is
  the same reading FluentCommandBar already had.
